From a7209a2e7e6e36412a011b2e45eb138930ad8fa9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xFFshell <0xffshell@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 13:11:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Put the eyes on the block a walk is for, on the approach

A walk to a chest, a door, a crop or a block to be mined arrives with the
head still on the line the path takes - the movement's own target, leaned
onto where the path goes next - and the process that wanted the block
turns it at the last moment: the bot walks up to a chest facing the wall
beside it and turns once the feet have stopped. That turn is the tell.

Over the last six blocks of a walk whose goal names a block the gaze is
now offered that block in place of the line ahead, into the same lean,
easing in over four blocks so nothing appears at a distance, at most half
of the way to it, fading out between forty and sixty degrees off the walk,
and nothing at all when the goal names no block, when the block is the one
the player already stands in, or when the walk is a fall, a climb, a jump,
a parkour run-up or in water. A target about to be clicked is still aimed
at exactly, and the lean is on the gaze only - the movement's own rotation
is untouched.

The rule is behavior/look/ApproachLook.java, free of Minecraft and driven
directly by ApproachLookTest (13 tests; the suite is 320 in 38 classes,
0 failures). Which block a goal is about is asked of the goal and not
guessed: only the goals that render as a position name one, a composite
answers with the part of itself nearest the end of the walk, and
PathExecutor resolves the answer once per path from the executing goal -
which, unlike the path's own goal, has not been simplified to a GoalXZ by
simplifyUnloadedYCoord, so a walk to a chest calculated from far off no
longer arrives having forgotten what it was for.

HUMANLIKE.md gains the row beside the lean it shares its slot with.
