+| The eyes are on the job before the feet get there | `behavior/look/ApproachLook.java`, `PathExecutor#lookAtTheJob` | A person who has decided to open a chest, mine an ore, use a table or work a field is looking at it while they walk the last steps to it: the eyes arrive with the feet and the only thing left to do is reach. A walk's own look is the walk - the movement's target for the tick, leaned onto the line the path takes after it (the row above) - so on a walk whose goal named a block the head stayed on the line until the feet had stopped and the process that wanted the block turned it at the last moment: the bot walked up to a chest facing the wall beside it and turned. Over the last six blocks of such a walk the gaze is now offered that block in place of the line ahead, easing in over four blocks so nothing appears at a distance, at most half of the way to it, and nothing at all once it sits more than forty degrees off the walk - fading out by sixty, the over-the-shoulder bound the row above already holds itself to - or when it is the block the feet are standing in, which is where every ordinary `goto` ends and is what keeps this from changing a walk that is not walking *to* something. The block is the goal's own, asked for and not guessed: only the goals that name one render as a position (`GoalBlock`, `GoalTwoBlocks`, `GoalGetToBlock`, `GoalNear`), a composite - what mining and exploring walk on - answers with the part of itself nearest the end of the walk, and the answer is resolved once per path from the executing goal rather than every tick, because `simplifyUnloadedYCoord` can hand the pathfinder a `GoalXZ` in place of the goal that named the block and a walk to a chest calculated from far off would otherwise arrive having forgotten what it was for. Offered into the same lean, so every rule of the row above holds unchanged: the lean is on the gaze only and the movement's own rotation is untouched, a fall, a climb, a jump, a parkour run-up or water get